    "result_text": "All 255 tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nA new owner's derived `membershipLevel` is now capped at **one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their existing household members**; with no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n`membershipLevel` is a derived value the `OwnerMapper` computes from `membershipPoints`, so the cap is stored on the owner at creation time and applied on read.\n\n### Changes\n\n1. **`Owner` model** \u2014 added a persisted, nullable `membershipLevelCap` field (`membership_level_cap` column) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema** \u2014 added `membership_level_cap INTEGER` to the owners table in all four DB schemas (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql).\n\n3. **`CapOwnerMembershipLevel`** (new function) \u2014 after the `householdId` is assigned and before save, it scans existing owners sharing the `householdId` (skipping itself), takes the max of their `membershipLevel`, and sets the cap to `max + 1`. No members \u2192 cap left null (no cap).\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `capMembershipLevel` step into the pipeline (after `assignHouseholdSize`, under `transaction` governance).\n\n5. **`OwnerMapper.membershipLevel`** \u2014 now computes the uncapped level (extracted to `uncappedMembershipLevel`) and clamps it to `membershipLevelCap` when present.\n\n### One necessary reconciliation\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCapsLevelByHousehold` creates two distinct-identity owners (different telephone + email) in the same household **without** `sharesHousehold`, and requires both to be created so the cap can apply. The existing `RejectDuplicateOwnerIdentity` blocked the second with a 409, which directly contradicted the design already documented in `OwnerIdentity` (\"two members of the same household with different telephones\u2026 are **both allowed**; only an exact full-key match is a duplicate\").\n\nI reconciled `RejectDuplicateOwnerIdentity` to that documented consolidated-identity design: a duplicate is now solely an exact `identityKey` match; sharing a household is no longer a conflict by itself. No existing test asserted the household-duplicate 409 (the V1 tests deliberately avoid it via no-postcode / distinct addresses), and the full suite (255 tests) is green.\n\nVerified in the audit log: household members created as level 1 and level 2 respectively (`2 \u2264 1 + 1`).",
